<h2>How the course runs</h2>
<p>Each week works through one chapter. The exercises at the end of each module are the best practice for the problem
  sets and the exams: try each derivation yourself before checking it against the notes, and change the code to see
  what breaks.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Problem sets (40%)</strong> — five sets, 8% each, mixing derivations with implementation. A short math
    diagnostic (HW0) in week 1 helps you find gaps in the prerequisites early.</li>
  <li><strong>Midterm exam (15%)</strong> — in week 8, covering modules 01–07.</li>
  <li><strong>Final exam (20%)</strong> — cumulative, with emphasis on modules 08–14.</li>
  <li><strong>Final project (25%)</strong> — research-style: a proposal (week 5), a milestone (week 10), a draft
    (week 14), a presentation (week 15), and a final report.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Getting set up</h2>
<p>The code in the notes needs Python 3 with NumPy and SciPy; nothing else. Any recent Python installation, or a free
  <a href="https://colab.research.google.com/" rel="noopener">Google Colab</a> notebook in the browser, runs every
  example. Paste the cells of a module into a notebook in order and run them: every random number comes from a seeded
  generator, so your output should match the notes.</p>

<h2>Course policies</h2>

<h3>Late work</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Three late days</strong> in total across all problem sets, no questions asked; each extends a deadline by 24 hours.</li>
  <li>Once late days are used up, late submissions lose 20% per day for up to three days, and receive no credit after that.</li>
  <li>Project deadlines are <strong>firm</strong>; late days do not apply.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Collaboration</h3>
<p>
  Discussing problem sets with classmates at the level of ideas is encouraged, but <strong>your code and write-ups must
  be your own</strong>. Helping someone debug an error message is fine; sharing solutions is not. Pair work is welcome on
  the final project, as long as the report makes each member's contribution clear.
</p>

<h3>AI and LLM use</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Allowed without disclosure:</strong> autocomplete-style help, syntax lookups, and debugging individual error messages.</li>
  <li><strong>Allowed with disclosure:</strong> explanations of concepts you are stuck on, and suggested approaches after you have thought the problem through. A one-line note in your submission is enough.</li>
  <li><strong>Not allowed:</strong> generating problem-set solutions or large blocks of project code with an LLM. Undisclosed LLM-generated work is an academic-integrity violation.</li>
</ul>
<p>When in doubt, disclose.</p>
